Report what VerifyFwSignatureBeforeDecrypt does, step by step.

DIAGNOSTIC PATCH.  It adds TRACE lines and changes no logic; every one is
under SFU_VERBOSE_DEBUG_MODE, so a non-verbose build is identical to stock.

VerifyFwSignatureBeforeDecrypt is static, so it cannot be called or
instrumented from outside sfu_fwimg_no_swap.c, and it collapses several
distinct outcomes -- SE_Decrypt_Init failed, SE_AuthenticateFW_Init failed,
the loop never entered, a chunk read failed, a decrypt failed, a hash
update failed, the tag did not match -- into one SFU_ERROR.  ST's caller
then prints "Fw signature check error", which names only the last of them
and is emitted for all of them.

On this port the symptom was: both SE init calls return SE_SUCCESS with
FwSize 102400 and chunk 64, the loop bound (512 < 102912) is plainly true,
the loop IS entered -- and it runs exactly one iteration.  Every component
works when called from main() against the same image.  The traces added
here bracket the loop and then each of the three steps inside it, for the
first two iterations only, so the trace cannot dominate the timing of the
pass it is reporting on.

GENERATED WITH diff, NOT BY HAND.  The first version of this patch was
hand-written and its hunk header understated the added lines; `patch`
applied PART of the hunk and reported success, so only one of the two
TRACEs existed -- and the missing one was the informative one.  That is the
failure the Makefile's -F0 is supposed to prevent and does not, because the
context still matched.  scratchpad/mkpatch.py regenerates this file, and
asserts that every anchor meant to fire once actually matched.

